Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:07.690 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  [Applause]  hi everyone  nice to see so many people here  i thought i'd talk a bit about the  campaign's free nazis gary radcliffe so  i'm tunis siddique i'm the mp for  hampstead and kilburn i got elected in  2015.  i inherited my seat from i think  inherited i didn't win it but the mp  before me was glenda jackson which many  of you will know  glenda jackson is 85 by the way and  she's back on stage and i speak to her  on the phone quite regularly for anyone  who's interested and she's full of  spirit as she always was swearing and  full of delight about taking down the  government so she's still in good  spirits so i got elected in 2015 in the  most marginal seats in the country after  i got elected i only won by a thousand  votes so fairly marginal seat  i had a baby in 2016 and i'll tell you  why this is relevant  so i had a baby in april on the 8th of  april little girl holds azalea and  believe it or not parliament doesn't  have any maternity leave nothing there's  nothing like you're meant to have a baby  have an emergency c-section like i did  go back to work the next stage that's  what you're meant to do i went back six  weeks later with your doctor's orders  when i was in hospital at the royal free  best hospital in the world in my  constituency  i had a call from my office manager the  day after  and ollie was very apologetic he called  me and he said look i know i'm not meant  to be calling you i know you've had a  dramatic birth but one of your  constituents has been detained in iran  and uh their wife the husband of the of  the person who's been detained has  turned up  to the office saying that they need to  speak to you and this is one of the  things about politics my office manager  is very good very experienced but if i'm  the mp and one of my constituents has  been detained in iran a british citizen  then it doesn't matter if i've just had  a baby or not i've got to deal with this  case  so i said to ali okay well why don't you  send richard the husband of the nazis  zagari radcliff who now has become  famous in his own right to my house i  had no other choice  so the first time i met richard  i was  breastfeeding my newborn i still had my  c-section scars and richard walked in  through the door  i said to him look what's going on and  he said my wife nazineen who is british  but also iranian so she's a british  passport and she's an iranian passport  travelled  she traveled with our 18 month old so  nazney had gone with their 18 month old  to visit her parents in tehran on a  holiday for two weeks  while she was there on the day she was  coming back she was detained at tehran  airport and she was told that she was  charged on  charges of espionage and arrested and  taken to even prison and the daughter  was given the little daughter was given  to the grandparents and i said to  richard right how was all this conveyed  to you and said well the parents told me  and then i went to the foreign office  and the foreign office had said to  richard  don't say anything so that said if you  say anything this will endanger your  wife we might not be able to bring her  back so stay quiet  i said to richard okay then why are you  here if the foreign officer said  don't say anything because if it's gonna  put your wife's life in danger  bear in mind i'd only been an mp for a  year at that point and i was very unsure  about what to do and richard said look  i've stayed quiet now for four weeks  they don't even know where my wife is  they don't know where she is whether  she's an even prison whether she's under  a house arrest they seem just satisfied  with the fact that they know  she's alive but how do they even know  she's alive so i said to richard look  you're making a very big decision here  have you decided that you're going  public with this he said yeah we're  launching a media campaign because  that's the only way to raise attention i  mean it's a very big risk and bear in  mind being a new mp and opposition mp i  really wasn't sure whether this was the  right approach do we go public about the  fact that this woman's been detained do  we um end up  endangering her life does it mean she  never comes back like i wasn't sure  so i rang jeremy corbyn because he was  the leader of labour party at the time  and i said jeremy you know iran well  what should we do about this and he said  okay um  call richard over to your house let's  meet bear in mind again i can't move  from the from my bed following my  emergency c-section so on the defining  moments of my career i have jeremy  corbyn  and richard radcliffe in my living room  with my newborn uh breastfeeding and  taking notes with the other hand on the  iranian revolutionary guards was you  know something i'll always remember so  jeremy's in my living room richard's in  my living room we're like what should we  do about this and  richard said to me there's something  bigger here there's no reason they would  pick up my wife a charity worker keeper  in tehran at this point they haven't  heard anything for six weeks about his  wife  i mean richard is an accountant his wife  is a charity worker and she's been  picked up at the states so he said no  we've got to go public  the minute i went back to parliament we  handed in a petition to david cameron at  the time  and we raised it in in parliament in a  debate the minute her name is gary  radcliffe's name was publicly mentioned  for the first time  in parliament things started moving  philip hammond at the time called us in  for a meeting david kevin received our  petition  the times wrote a little piece about it  and i said to richard look this is  taking off are you sure this is not  endangering your wife's life and you  know i was quite uncertain like i'm not  the foreign secretary i really wasn't  sure what the right approach was he was  absolutely adamant and you know i pay  tribute to this man from west hampton  accountant who said  this is what we've got to do so what  followed is a long series of hunger  strikes and  singing carols handing in petitions  raising it at every opportunity in the  chamber so i raised it with five foreign  secretaries i raised them with three  prime ministers with david cameron with  theresa may with boris johnson  constantly racing and the news to be  fair kept it in the cycle all the time  finally richard got to speak to nazalene  and that was after three months as she  was in solitary confinement when he  spoke to her  he basically said to her is this the  right thing is this the right thing to  be doing going public with the fact that  they've held you  hostage effectively and she said richard  i'm paying the price of a historic debt  and he said what do you mean he said  the country so the uk oduran 400 million  pounds which was a historic debt  dating back to the 1970s where we  purchased some tanks from them and um  they registered some times for us paid  us the money we never gave them the tax  but we never gave the money back that's  basically what happened 400 million  pounds  and richard said to dazny how do you  know that's why you're being held and  she said  the people who are interrogating me told  me they told her from the second week of  her being in prison that the reason she  was being held is not because of  espionage they never said she was guilty  of any crime and this is a reoccurring  theme because i had meetings with the  iranian ambassador as well at no point  did he ever say your constituent is  guilty of this this and this never he  never said she was guilty of anything he  basically said if you give us the money  back you will get your constituent back  so i said to richard i mean this is a  ridiculous situation why has she paid  the price for the money that we as a  country owe iran and he said she's a  pawn caught between the two countries  but she's not the only one  so there were other british nationals  who were captured in iran but their  families had decided  that they weren't going to go public for  fear of endangering their you know  family member i mean i understand that  completely understanding but richard  said to me now we've got to go public on  it so we started raising it more and  more whether it was parliamentary  questions and this is some this is a  campaign where i had to work cross-party  and i know in parliament when normally  when you look at the headlines and you  read the newspapers it's a lot about us  working in silo i mean from the labour  party do i work just with labour party  mps i knew i wasn't going to get  anything done i had to reach across the  political divide no matter what my  differences is so i started talking to  oliver dowden because he had the  conservative mp he had a constituent in  iran as well kamal ferrugi he was  captured there so i said to oliver look  why don't we go together to number 10 to  see theresa may and say look what are we  going to do about getting our  constituents out and actually he agreed  so we went together so this is one of  those things where conservative mp  started working with me because at the  heart of it was real injustice this  woman she'd done nothing wrong we hadn't  paid the price of um this 400 million  pounds and she was in solitary  confinement  separated from her husband separated  from her child and locked up in in even  prison for no reason  lots of things happened during the six  years but we got small victories the  first victory was that she was not  allowed a medical examination she was  going on hunger strike her hair was  falling out she had lumps on her breasts  she wasn't allowed to make medical  examination so richard said to me we've  got to stage basically a protest on this  so we did a massive protest after which  we must have put pressure on the foreign  office who then put pressure on the  iranians and she was allowed in medical  examination so we were like okay it's  obviously worse  the next lobbying that we wanted to do  was to allow her  um phone calls twice a week to her  family in england but also to see her  little daughter who by the way was still  in iran had forgotten how to speak  english and was being raised by her  grandparents speaking farsi  she was then allowed to visit with her  daughter the first time her little  daughter went to see her in prison she  had to put a sack over her head then she  saw her mother and couldn't believe her  mother was alive so it was real human  impact on a small child as well  after that the next thing richard said  to me is that we've got to go i'm going  to go on hunger strike i'll be honest he  asked me to go on hunger strike as well  i said  you're a step too far there but i'll let  you go from just right he went to the  hunger strike we were outside the  iranian embassy they wouldn't open the  door to us but that seemed to have  turned a corner as well after the hunger  strike and i think the foreign office  really realized something was happening  here they called us in they gave us  another update but all the while they  kept saying to us don't make this  campaign public don't talk about nazim  in the press don't talk about this don't  talk about that there's always hush hush  we're doing some mediation behind the  scenes when negotiating with them we'll  bring her back don't say anything  to continue watching this video click  the link in the top left or in the  description below or visit iai.tv for  more debates and talks from the world's  leading thinkers on today's biggest  ideas

